                                                          5
    The preceding lemmas identify the optimal candidate in the family K(n, p, q). We now turn to
the structural reduction that explains why this family is extremal. The natural parameter in this
reduction is the incidence rank of the complement. For a bipartite graph H with bipartition U ∪ V ,
its bipartite incidence matrix is the 0–1 matrix whose rows are indexed by U , whose columns are
indexed by V , and whose (u, v)-entry is one if and only if uv ∈ E(H). The rank of this matrix over
R is called the incidence rank of H. The bipartite graph H is a chain graph if the vertices of U
can be ordered as u1 , . . . , up so that

                                 NH (u1 ) ⊆ NH (u2 ) ⊆ · · · ⊆ NH (up ).

Equivalently, the vertices in the two bipartition classes can be ordered so that every row of the
bipartite incidence matrix consists of an initial block of ones followed by zeros, with the block
lengths nondecreasing down the rows. Such a matrix is called a Ferrers matrix. We use this
ordering throughout. The following lemma provides a sufficient algebraic condition for a graph to
have a chain graph as its complement.

Lemma 2.6. Let B be a symmetric 0–1 matrix with diagonal entries one. Suppose that there exist
vectors x > 0 and y such that, for u ̸= v, Buv = 1 exactly when xu xv + yu yv ≥ 0. Then the graph
with adjacency matrix J − B is a chain graph.

Proof. Put ru = yu /xu . Since xu > 0, for u ̸= v, Buv = 0 exactly when 1 + ru rv < 0. Thus every
edge of the graph with adjacency matrix J − B joins a vertex with positive ru to one with negative
ru , while vertices with ru = 0 are isolated. If ru , rv have the same nonzero sign, |ru | ≤ |rv |, and w
is adjacent to u, then rw has the opposite sign and rv rw ≤ ru rw < −1. Hence every neighbor of u is
also a neighbor of v. Ordering the vertices on each side by |ru | therefore orders their neighborhoods
by inclusion, so the graph is a chain graph.

  We now apply this threshold condition to establish the main structural reduction for an edge-
maximal extremal graph.

Proposition 2.7. Let G be an n-vertex graph maximizing S2 (G), chosen among all such graphs
to have the maximum number of edges. Then G is connected and G is a chain graph.

Proof. Let x, y be any orthonormal eigenvectors of A(G) corresponding to λ1 (G) and λ2 (G), with
vertex coordinates xu , yu .
    If uv ∈/ E(G), Lemma 2.1 gives S2 (G + uv) ≥ S2 (G) + 2(xu xv + yu yv ). Hence xu xv + yu yv ≤ 0.
Equality would produce a maximizer with more edges, contrary to the choice of G, so the inequality
is strict. Similarly, deleting an edge uv gives S2 (G − uv) ≥ S2 (G) − 2(xu xv + yu yv ), and hence
xu xv + yu yv ≥ 0. Thus uv ∈ E(G) exactly when xu xv + yu yv ≥ 0.
    Suppose that G is disconnected. Since A(G) is block diagonal and the preceding argument
applies to any such pair of eigenvectors, we may choose each eigenvector to be supported on one
component. If their supporting components are distinct, choose one vertex from each; otherwise
choose one vertex inside their common supporting component and one outside it. In either case,
we obtain vertices u, v in distinct components with xu xv + yu yv = 0. The preceding criterion would
make uv an edge, a contradiction. Hence G is connected. By the Perron–Frobenius theorem, we
may now choose x > 0. The same criterion and Lemma 2.6, applied to B(G), show that G is a
chain graph.

   We now organize the argument by the incidence rank of G. Rank zero means that G is empty,
so G = Kn = K(n, 0, 0). If the incidence rank is one, all nonzero rows of the bipartite incidence


                                                   6
matrix are equal: indeed, they are scalar multiples of one another, and two nonzero 0–1 rows can be
scalar multiples only with scalar one. Hence G is a complete bipartite graph together with isolated
vertices, and therefore G ∼= K(n, p, q) for some p, q > 0 with p + q ≤ n. Proposition 2.3 therefore
settles the cases of incidence rank at most one. It remains to exclude incidence rank at least two,
which is the purpose of the technical estimates developed in the next section.


3    Technical estimates and proof of the main theorem
The exclusion of chain complements of incidence rank at least two rests on two estimates: a spectral-
tail inequality for weighted Ferrers quotients and a uniform numerical defect. To prove the first, we
compress a chain graph to its nonzero neighborhood classes and use the sign pattern of the inverse
quotient to control coefficient sign changes.
    For a finite sequence (a0 , . . . , am ) with no zero terms, let var(a0 , . . . , am ) be the number of
indices j ∈ {1, . . . , m} such that aj−1 aj < 0. These indices are called the sign-change positions. A
real matrix is totally nonnegative if all its minors are nonnegative. The following is the zero-free
specialization of the variation-diminishing theorem [14, Theorem 3.4(a)].
Lemma 3.1. Let M be totally nonnegative. If x and M x have no zero coordinates, then var(M x) ≤
var(x).
    We now use this property to bound the displacement of sign-change positions.
