    Let H be a bipartite chain graph of incidence rank k ≥ 1. Deleting zero rows and zero columns
from a bipartite incidence matrix does not change its rank. Since H is a chain graph, its distinct
nonzero row supports are linearly ordered by inclusion; write ∅ = R0 ⊊ R1 ⊊ · · · ⊊ Rℓ . For
each 1 ≤ i ≤ ℓ, choose vi ∈ Ri \ Ri−1 . The submatrix whose rows are the distinct rows with
supports R1 , . . . , Rℓ and whose columns are v1 , . . . , vℓ has (i, j)-entry one if and only if j ≤ i. It is
therefore lower triangular with diagonal entries one, so these ℓ rows are linearly independent. Since
every nonzero row is equal to one of them, they also span the row space. Hence ℓ = k, and thus
∅ = R0 ⊊ R1 ⊊ · · · ⊊ Rk . For 1 ≤ i ≤ k, let pi be the number of rows with support Ri , and put
qi = |Ri \ Ri−1 |. Define
