   With these matrices defined, we now show that the spectrum of J − A(H) reduces to that of
the smaller quotient matrix M0 or Mc .

Lemma 3.3. Let H be a bipartite chain graph of incidence rank k ≥ 1. If c = 0, the spectrum of
J − A(H) consists of the spectrum of M0 together with zeros. If c > 0, it consists of the spectrum
of Mc together with zeros.

Proof. Partition the vertices of H into the row blocks of sizes p1 , . . . , pk , the column blocks of sizes
q1 , . . . , qk , and, when c > 0, the block of isolated vertices. Let U ⊆ RV (H) be the subspace√of vectors
