   For a polynomial P (t) =          j=0 aj t with no zero coefficients, write var(P ) = var(a0 , . . . , am ).

Lemma 3.4. Let P be real-rooted and have no zero root or zero coefficient. Then var(P ) equals
the number of positive roots of P , counted with multiplicity.

Proof. The coefficient of tj in P (−t) is (−1)j aj . Thus, at each position, exactly one of the coefficient
sequences of P (t) and P (−t) has a sign change. Hence

                                         var(P ) + var(P (−t)) = deg P.

Descartes’ rule of signs [19, Theorem] bounds the numbers of positive roots of P (t) and P (−t) by
these two variations, respectively. These are the numbers of positive and negative roots of P . Since
all roots of P are real and nonzero, their sum is deg P , so both bounds are equalities.

      The inertia of a real symmetric matrix is the triple (n+ , n− , n0 ) giving the numbers of its posi-
tive, negative, and zero eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity. In the next lemma, the parameters
pi , qi , c in (11) are allowed to be arbitrary positive real numbers.

